\title{Clean: Circuits and Their Specifications in Lean}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-24}
\meta{toc}{true}

\p{
    An embedded [Lean](dhsorens-lean) DSL for writing zk circuits against [AIR](dhsorens-002U),
    [Plonkish](dhsorens-002T), and [R1CS](dhsorens-002S), built by zkSecurity under a Verified-zkEVM
    grant. Circuit, specification, and proof live in the same file. The constraints of such a
    circuit cut out an [algebraic variety](dhsorens-0055) in the witness cells: a satisfying
    assignment is a point on that variety, and the spec is what that point is supposed to mean.
}

\p{
    The object that carries the correctness claim is \code{FormalCircuit}, and its content is a
    contract. A circuit is bundled with an \code{Assumptions} predicate on its inputs, a \code{Spec}
    relating inputs to outputs, and proofs of two statements: \em{[soundness](dhsorens-004Z)}, that
    the constraints together with the assumptions imply the spec, and
    \em{[completeness](dhsorens-004T)}, that the assumptions imply the circuit's own witness
    generators satisfy the constraints. Taken together they say the circuit behaves as a function
    with \code{Assumptions} as precondition and \code{Spec} as postcondition.
}

\p{
    What makes the arrangement scale is that subcircuits enter proofs through their specs rather
    than their constraints. \code{ConstraintsHold.Soundness} replaces each subcircuit in the
    operation list by the implication from its assumptions to its spec, and the completeness
    counterpart replaces it by its prover-side assumptions — so a gadget is proved once and
    thereafter used as a black box. The 200,000-constraint SHA-256 circuit in the
    [zk.golf](dhsorens-0053) repository is proved this way, as a composition of some twenty gadget
    circuits each carrying its own soundness and completeness theorem, none of them reproved at the
    top level.
}

\p{
    Clean and [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) prove complementary halves of a proof system's correctness and
    do not currently compose (but may do in the future). ArkLib's theorems are about a protocol over
    a constraint system: given that the system is satisfiable, the argument is knowledge-sound.
    Clean's theorems are about what satisfiability of that system means for the computation. The
    protocol theorem assumes the arithmetisation says what it is meant to say, and the circuit
    theorem is what discharges that assumption — but joining them is an exploratory item on Clean's
    roadmap, not a result. This is the load-bearing seam between the two libraries, and it is still
    open.
}

\transclude{dhsorens-004Y} % circuits, constraints, witness generation
\transclude{dhsorens-004Z} % soundness and completeness of a circuit
\transclude{dhsorens-0050} % arithmetisations and backends
\transclude{dhsorens-0051} % circuits in Clean
\transclude{dhsorens-0052} % interfaces as specifications

